The sector buffer is a true dual-port RAM, and must be written as one

scsi_targ's 512-byte sector buffer has two ports -- the SCSI side and the
block back end -- and puts all four accesses in a single always_ff block.
That is not the shape either vendor's inference engine recognises for a true
dual-port RAM, and Quartus quietly builds the whole thing out of flip-flops
instead.  Measured on a MAX 10 10M50, in a Sun-2/50 with the VME SCSI board
fitted:

    scsi_targ:drive     11,471 logic elements    4,352 registers    0 memory bits

4,352 registers is the 512 x 8 array plus its state; 11,471 logic elements is
most of a fifth of the device, spent on a buffer that fits in one M9K with
4,096 of its 8,192 bits left over.  It is not reported as an uninferred RAM,
so nothing in the log points at it -- the only symptom is a build that is
suddenly 12,000 elements larger, and on this device that is the difference
between 58% and 83% full.

Splitting it into one block per port is the documented template for both
Quartus and Vivado.  Nothing observable changes: the two ports never address
the same byte at the same time, because the target either fills the buffer
from the media or drains it to the SCSI bus and never does both at once, so
the simultaneous-write case that a single block resolves by assignment order
cannot arise here.

Worth sending upstream: the model is otherwise entirely synthesisable, and
this is the only thing in it that costs a device more than it should.

--- a/src/scsi_targ.sv
+++ b/src/scsi_targ.sv
@@ -138,10 +138,25 @@
     blk_o.buf_rdata = sbuf_rdata;
   end
 
+  // One always block per port, which is the shape a synthesiser looks for in a
+  // true dual-port RAM.  With all four accesses in a single block Quartus
+  // declines to infer one and builds the whole 512 bytes out of flip-flops --
+  // measured at 11,471 logic elements and 4,352 registers on a MAX 10, against
+  // 4,096 bits of an M9K that is otherwise idle, which is most of a fifth of
+  // the device for a sector buffer.
+  //
+  // The two ports never address the same byte at once: the target either fills
+  // the buffer from the media or drains it to the SCSI bus, never both.  So
+  // splitting the block changes nothing that can be observed, and the
+  // simultaneous-write case a single block resolves by assignment order does
+  // not arise.
   always_ff @(posedge clk_i) begin
     if (a_we) mem[a_addr] <= a_wdata;
+    a_rdata <= mem[a_addr];
+  end
+
+  always_ff @(posedge clk_i) begin
     if (blk_i.buf_we) mem[blk_i.buf_addr] <= blk_i.buf_wdata;
-    a_rdata    <= mem[a_addr];
     sbuf_rdata <= mem[blk_i.buf_addr];
   end
 
